(window.GROUP_QUIZZES=window.GROUP_QUIZZES||{})["interpretation-of-medical-literature-exam-1-medical-literature-master-exam-form-b"]={"title":"Medical Literature Master Exam — Form B","category":"Interpretation of Medical Literature","exam":"Exam 1","sem":"fall-2026","questions":[{"q":"Which finding worsens prognosis after an acute myocardial infarction?","choices":["Normal blood pressure","Never having smoked","Congestive heart failure","Younger age"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct, one of the poor prognostic factors, alongside ventricular arrhythmia, anterior infarction, hypotension and increasing age. — 7 PAJ5512 Prognosis MBF 2026sc.pptx, Slide 28"},{"q":"Which challenge concerns how research findings are communicated?","choices":["Findings are only published in languages other than English","Findings may be presented in language clinicians struggle with","Findings are published too frequently to keep up with","Findings are withheld by journals until they are outdated"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct, and it is a distinct problem from the evidence being absent or conflicting: the evidence exists and is sound, but does not reach the person who needs it. — 2 PAJ5512 Evidence MBF_2026sc.pptx, Slide 6"},{"q":"With a sensitivity of 99 percent and specificity of 95 percent, positive predictive value rises from 17 to 51 percent as prevalence rises from one to five percent. What does that demonstrate?","choices":["The test became more accurate","Negative predictive value is unaffected by prevalence","Specificity rises with prevalence","Predictive value depends heavily on prevalence"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct, and the test itself did not change: the same sensitivity and specificity produced a threefold difference in what a positive result means. — 4 PAJ5512 Tests MBF 2026sc.pptx, Slide 28"},{"q":"What happens to a censored patient in a Kaplan-Meier analysis?","choices":["Is replaced by a newly enrolled patient","Is counted as alive until the end of the study","Leaves the number at risk without counting as a death","Is counted as a death at that time"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct: a censored patient is no longer followed, so the number at risk falls without a death being recorded. — 7 PAJ5512 Prognosis MBF 2026sc.pptx, Slide 18"},{"q":"What is the Hawthorne effect?","choices":["Condition changes because a change is expected","Behavior changes when given special attention","Participants receive an unrecorded second treatment","Participants stop treatment after side effects"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct: participants change their behavior because of the special interest or attention they receive during a study. — 8 PAJ5512 Research and Trials MBF_2026sc.pptx, Slide 21"},{"q":"What is a variable called when its presence or absence changes the exposure's effect?","choices":["A confounder","A crossover variable","An effect modifier","A selection factor"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct: the exposure acts differently depending on the variable, which is not the same as merely distorting the association. — 8 PAJ5512 Research and Trials MBF_2026sc.pptx, Slide 31"},{"q":"Overdiagnosis is an extreme example of which screening bias?","choices":["Lead-time bias","Length-time bias","Compliance bias","Recall bias"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Screening picks up the slowest conditions, and at the extreme they are so indolent that finding them does not help the patient. — 8 PAJ5512 Prevention MBF_2026sc.pptx, Slide 23"},{"q":"What defines an experimental design?","choices":["Groups are defined by their outcome","Participants are followed forward over time","The investigator assigns the intervention","The exposure is observed rather than assigned"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct, and the randomized controlled trial is the example. Where the exposure is merely observed rather than assigned, the design is observational. The investigator manipulates it as well. — 2 PAJ5512 Study Design and Research Methods MBF_2026sc.pptx, Slide 9"},{"q":"A well-designed randomized trial finds no effect of an exposure on an outcome. What does that provide?","choices":["Strong evidence for a causal link","Strong evidence against a causal link","Weak evidence against a causal link","No evidence either way"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct, because a strong design combined with a finding of no effect gives strong evidence against cause, provided the trial had adequate power. — 9 PAJ5512 Statistics Causation MBF_2026sc.pptx, Slide 35"},{"q":"A healthy patient at raised risk starts a daily medication solely to lower the chance of future disease. Which type of clinical prevention is this?","choices":["Immunization","Screening","Behavioral or lifestyle counseling","Chemoprevention"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Giving a drug to healthy people to reduce their chance of developing disease is the chemoprevention type. — 8 PAJ5512 Prevention MBF_2026sc.pptx, Slide 4"},{"q":"Why does it matter whether a test study's prevalence matches your own population?","choices":["The gold standard will classify patients differently","The likelihood ratio will change","Sensitivity will differ if prevalence differs","Predictive values will differ if prevalence differs"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct, and that is the practical consequence of predictive values depending on prevalence: a published value may not transfer to your setting. — 4 PAJ5512 Tests MBF 2026sc.pptx, Slide 50"},{"q":"Written in the lettered cells, what is sensitivity?","choices":["d over c plus d","a over a plus b","a over a plus c","d over b plus d"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct, since a is true positives and c false negatives, so the denominator is everyone who actually has the disease. — Assigned reading: The Classic 2 X 2 Table"},{"q":"What is the particular value of a disease registry?","choices":["It provides information about trends over time","It establishes the cause of a disease directly","It measures self-reported behavior in a representative sample","It registers every birth and death in the jurisdiction"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Registries are usually established by state government to collect disease-specific data, and because collection continues, they show how a disease behaves across years rather than at one moment. — 1 PAJ5512 Introduction Bias Validity MBF_2026sc.pptx, Slide 16"},{"q":"What is the denominator of the maternal mortality rate?","choices":["The mid-interval population of women","Live births in the same interval","Total deaths from all causes","The number of pregnancies in the interval"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct, multiplied by 100,000, with deaths assigned to pregnancy-related causes in the numerator. — 3 PAJ5512 Rates and Measurement MBF_2026sc.pptx, Slide 9"},{"q":"Which rates are named as specific to maternal and child health?","choices":["Incidence and prevalence rates","Crude and age-specific death rates","Live birth rates and fetal, infant and maternal death rates","Case fatality and death-to-case ratio"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct, with neonatal and perinatal death rates alongside them, all of which take live births rather than population as their denominator. — 3 PAJ5512 Rates and Measurement MBF_2026sc.pptx, Slide 6"},{"q":"Which resource incorporates InfoPOEMS and InfoRetriever?","choices":["Essential Evidence Plus","Medline","Up-To-Date","The Cochrane Library"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct, and it sits among the evidence-based databases with TripPro and Clinical Key rather than among the general literature indexes. — 2 PAJ5512 Evidence MBF_2026sc.pptx, Slide 22"},{"q":"What is prognosis?","choices":["A prediction of the course of a disease","The identification of a disease from its findings","The chance a healthy person develops a disease","The cause of a disease in one patient"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct, and it is what a clinician tries to estimate once a diagnosis is made, in terms such as survival, complications or disability. — 7 PAJ5512 Prognosis MBF 2026sc.pptx, Slide 4"},{"q":"What does the example of a sickle cell trait carrier illustrate?","choices":["Statistical and clinical abnormality always coincide","An abnormal finding can exist without the person being sick","A normal finding can coexist with disease","Extreme values are always undesirable"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct, which is one half of a pair: the other is that a normal test does not necessarily rule disease out. — 3 PAJ5512 Rates and Measurement MBF_2026sc.pptx, Slide 75"},{"q":"What limits how finely continuous data can be recorded?","choices":["The number of categories chosen in advance","The precision of the measurement","The size of the sample","The type of distribution the data follow"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. The underlying quantity varies continuously, but what can be recorded is bounded by the instrument and the method used to read it. — 3 PAJ5512 Rates and Measurement MBF_2026sc.pptx, Slide 51"},{"q":"Which kind of measurement error shows itself as a lack of validity?","choices":["Systematic error","Random error","Intrasubject variation","Interobserver variation"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct, because a flaw in the process pushes every reading the same way, so the measure is consistently not measuring what it should. — 3 PAJ5512 Rates and Measurement MBF_2026sc.pptx, Slide 65"},{"q":"Which is an unintentional cause of noncompliance?","choices":["Deciding the treatment is not needed","Skipping doses after feeling better","Stopping the drug out of distrust","Lack of transportation to visits"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct: noncompliance is not always intentional, and misunderstandings, lack of transportation and forgetfulness are examples. — 8 PAJ5512 Research and Trials MBF_2026sc.pptx, Slide 22"},{"q":"How does effect modification differ from confounding?","choices":["It always weakens the effect","It is a bias that must be removed","It arises only in randomized trials","It is a real effect, not a bias"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct: confounding is a systematic error to be controlled, whereas effect modification is not a bias but a genuine change in the effect of exposure. — 7 PAJ5512 Risk MBF 2026sc.pptx, Slide 28"},{"q":"A small trial of a drug that truly works reports a p value of 0.30. Which explanation is most plausible?","choices":["A type I error has occurred","Too little power to detect the difference","The null hypothesis has been proven true","The drug has been proven ineffective"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct: a small study has a low probability of detecting a real difference, so a false negative (type II error) is likely. — 9 PAJ5512 Statistics Causation MBF_2026sc.pptx, Slide 16"},{"q":"What does the number needed to harm measure?","choices":["Patients treated for one to benefit","Patients receiving an intervention for one to be harmed","Patients followed for one to be lost","Patients screened for one false-positive result"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. It counts how many patients must have a given intervention for one of them to experience a harm. — 8 PAJ5512 Prevention MBF_2026sc.pptx, Slide 14"},{"q":"What is the Framingham Heart Study an example of?","choices":["A prevalence, or cross-sectional, study","A randomized controlled trial","A case-control study","An incidence, or cohort, study"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. It began in 1948 with an original cohort, follows multiple generations and additional cohorts, and helped identify the cardiovascular risk factors. — 3 PAJ5512 Rates and Measurement MBF_2026sc.pptx, Slide 25"},{"q":"A clinician advises a patient to stop smoking to lower the chance of lung cancer. Which use of risk information is this?","choices":["Prevention","Cause","Prediction","Diagnosis"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct, because removing a risk factor lowers the probability of developing the disease, as with controlling hypertension to prevent heart failure or avoiding second-hand smoke. — 7 PAJ5512 Risk MBF 2026sc.pptx, Slide 7"},{"q":"What follows from the fact that a study has the potential for bias?","choices":["The study's conclusions can be accepted without further scrutiny","Statistical analysis will correct for it","Bias should be considered, but potential does not always mean it is present","The study's conclusions must be rejected"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct, and the practical consequence is that bias is often detectable when reading an article, so the reader's job is to look for it rather than to assume it either way. — 1 PAJ5512 Introduction Bias Validity MBF_2026sc.pptx, Slide 38"},{"q":"A study reports a relative risk of 1.2 with a 95 percent confidence interval of 0.8 to 1.8. What does that show?","choices":["No statistically significant difference","A statistically significant protective effect","A proven 20 percent increase in risk","A precise estimate of harm"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct: the interval contains 1, the value of no difference, so a true relative risk of 1 cannot be excluded. — 9 PAJ5512 Statistics Causation MBF_2026sc.pptx, Slide 15"},{"q":"A reviewer leaves out a trial with only a handful of participants. Which question about sampling the best studies guided this?","choices":["Was the research peer-reviewed?","Is the information still current?","Was the sample size sufficient?","What were the limitations?"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct, because a study too small to give a reliable estimate is weighed against the others when the best studies are sampled. — 10 PAJ5512 Clinical Questions and Reviews MBF_2026sc.pptx, Slide 19"},{"q":"What did Archie Cochrane advocate?","choices":["Randomized controlled trials and systematic reviews","The use of expert opinion to settle clinical disputes","Grading recommendations from A through D and I","The core concepts and definition of evidence-based medicine"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct, and he advocated them specifically as tools for verifying whether a treatment actually works, which is the question the whole field is built around. — 2 PAJ5512 Evidence MBF_2026sc.pptx, Slide 4"},{"q":"Why must the results of a randomized trial be applicable to a larger population?","choices":["A finding confined to the sample cannot guide practice","It allows the trial to be conducted more cheaply","It removes the need for a control group","Journals will not publish a trial without it"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct, and it is external validity by another name, which is why adequate sample size and a clear protocol are listed alongside it as requirements. — 2 PAJ5512 Study Design and Research Methods MBF_2026sc.pptx, Slide 30"},{"q":"On what basis does Grade D recommend against a service?","choices":["High certainty that the net benefit is moderate","Moderate or high certainty of no net benefit","At least moderate certainty that the net benefit is small","Insufficient evidence to determine the balance"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct, and the instruction is to discourage its use. This is a positive finding against the service rather than an absence of evidence about it. Harms outweighing benefits earns the same grade. — 2 PAJ5512 Evidence MBF_2026sc.pptx, Slide 13"},{"q":"Why do cohort studies usually use relative risk rather than odds ratio?","choices":["Cohort studies lack an unexposed group","Odds cannot be calculated from cohort data","Odds ratios apply only to randomized trials","Incidence can be calculated directly"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct: cohort studies can directly calculate the incidence of disease in the exposed versus non-exposed, so relative risk is usually used rather than the odds ratio. — 7 PAJ5512 Risk MBF 2026sc.pptx, Slide 35"},{"q":"Which question contains all four components of a well-built clinical question?","choices":["In adults with migraine, does daily propranolol, compared with placebo, reduce headache days?","Does daily propranolol, compared with placebo, work better than other treatments?","In adults with migraine, does daily propranolol reduce the number of headache days?","In adults with migraine, is propranolol better, compared with placebo, in some way?"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct, because it names the patients (adults with migraine), intervention (propranolol), comparison (placebo) and outcome (headache days). — 10 PAJ5512 Clinical Questions and Reviews MBF_2026sc.pptx, Slide 8"},{"q":"What is cross-over in a trial?","choices":["Leaving the trial before the outcome is measured","Switching from one assigned treatment to another","Receiving an unrecorded additional treatment","Entering the study without the disease"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct, and it is a methodological problem because participants no longer stay in the group to which they were randomly assigned. — 8 PAJ5512 Research and Trials MBF_2026sc.pptx, Slide 24"},{"q":"How does publication bias differ from reporting bias?","choices":["Publication bias concerns whole studies not being published","Reporting bias concerns whole studies not being published","Both concern errors in how participants were assigned","Both are removed by a larger sample size"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct, because non-significant research is less likely to be published, whereas reporting bias concerns only a subset of a study's data being made available. — 1 PAJ5512 Introduction Bias Validity MBF_2026sc.pptx, Slide 45"},{"q":"Why is the generalizability of a prognosis study often limited?","choices":["Its follow-up is always too short","Its outcomes cannot be measured","Its patients were always randomized","Its patients may not represent others"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct: they may also not represent an individual patient, so how similar they are to yours must be judged. — 7 PAJ5512 Prognosis MBF 2026sc.pptx, Slide 26"},{"q":"A program urges smokers to stop. Which objective does it serve?","choices":["Find disease so treatment prevents complications","Find disease so treatment can cure it","Change a behavioral risk factor","Change a metabolic risk factor"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Trying to get people to stop smoking is the standard example of targeting a behavioral risk factor in order to change the behavior. — 8 PAJ5512 Prevention MBF_2026sc.pptx, Slide 26"},{"q":"What does validity mean for a test?","choices":["Its ability to determine correctly who has the disease","Its ability to change the post-test probability","Its cost relative to the gold standard","Getting a similar result on repetition"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct, and it is measured by sensitivity and specificity. Reliability, by contrast, is getting a similar result when the test is repeated under the same conditions. — 4 PAJ5512 Tests MBF 2026sc.pptx, Slide 8"},{"q":"What is the ecologic fallacy?","choices":["Applying individual data to a whole population","Measuring exposure and outcome at the same time","Assuming an association proves causation","Applying aggregate data to an individual"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct, and it is the standing hazard of ecologic data: a relationship that holds between populations need not hold within any one person in them. — 2 PAJ5512 Study Design and Research Methods MBF_2026sc.pptx, Slide 19"},{"q":"What is the purpose of exploratory research?","choices":["To address a new or understudied topic and satisfy curiosity","To attempt to explain why something occurs","To analyze data that another researcher has already collected","To observe carefully and describe what is found"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct, and it has two further purposes that are practical rather than intellectual: testing the feasibility of a more careful study and developing the methods that study would use. — 1 PAJ5512 Introduction Bias Validity MBF_2026sc.pptx, Slide 33"},{"q":"What do near-identical five-year survival rates fail to show?","choices":["How many patients were studied","When the deaths occur","Which patients had the disease","Whether the disease exists"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct: conditions such as a dissecting aneurysm, lung cancer and amyotrophic lateral sclerosis can share one five-year rate while their survival curves differ greatly. — 7 PAJ5512 Prognosis MBF 2026sc.pptx, Slide 13"},{"q":"In which direction does a case-control study look?","choices":["Forward, from an assigned intervention to outcome","Backward, from outcome to exposure","At a single moment, with neither before the other","Forward, from exposure to outcome"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct: participants are sorted by whether the outcome is present, and each group is then examined for whether the risk factor was present beforehand. — 2 PAJ5512 Study Design and Research Methods MBF_2026sc.pptx, Slide 23"},{"q":"Which is a recognized source of treatment hypotheses?","choices":["Epidemiologic studies","A single coincidental recovery","A comparison of biased groups","Hope that a new drug works"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct, alongside the mechanism or etiology of the disease, clinical observation, and trial and error. — 8 PAJ5512 Research and Trials MBF_2026sc.pptx, Slide 7"},{"q":"Which instruments are used in primary research?","choices":["Questionnaires, surveys, measurements and lab results","Published guidelines and expert consensus statements","Disease registries maintained by state governments","Meta-analyses and systematic reviews"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct, and what they have in common is that the investigator generates the data rather than drawing on data someone else has already collected. — 2 PAJ5512 Study Design and Research Methods MBF_2026sc.pptx, Slide 3"},{"q":"Which question tests the content validity of a depression screening scale?","choices":["Does the scale give the same score on a second administration?","Does a high scorer have the affect of a depressed person?","Would a clinician interviewing the patient reach the same conclusion?","Are the items representative of accepted symptoms of depression?"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct, and the sample items make it concrete: sadness lasting more than two weeks, irritability and difficulty sleeping. — 3 PAJ5512 Rates and Measurement MBF_2026sc.pptx, Slide 54"},{"q":"What effect does awareness of an epidemic have?","choices":["It reduces the true incidence of the disease","It converts an epidemic into a pandemic","It has no effect on case finding","It can make new cases easier to diagnose"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct, with early COVID-19 as the example. It is also a reason measured frequency can rise partly because clinicians started looking. — 3 PAJ5512 Rates and Measurement MBF_2026sc.pptx, Slide 33"},{"q":"In a study of coffee drinking and pancreatic cancer, smoking is more common among coffee drinkers and also raises cancer risk. What role does smoking play?","choices":["The outcome","An effect modifier","A confounder","The exposure"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct: smoking is related to both the exposure, coffee, and the outcome, cancer, so it can produce a false association, a systematic error. — 7 PAJ5512 Risk MBF 2026sc.pptx, Slide 26"},{"q":"Before concluding that an association is causal, what must be excluded?","choices":["Temporality, strength and analogy","Bias, chance and confounding","Precision, power and validity","Dose-response, reversibility and specificity"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct, taken in that order: bias in selection or measurement, then chance, then confounding, and only if none explains it is a cause likely. — 9 PAJ5512 Statistics Causation MBF_2026sc.pptx, Slide 29"},{"q":"What happens after peer reviewers recommend acceptance?","choices":["The authors repeat the study","It is published with no further review","Peer reviewers correct the grammar","Editors review language and grammar"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct, and only then is the article published; peer review as a whole helps to counter conflict of interest. — 10 PAJ5512 Clinical Questions and Reviews MBF_2026sc.pptx, Slide 34"},{"q":"A program finds mostly slow-growing cancers, and screened patients appear to outlive unscreened patients whose cancers were found by symptoms. Which bias best explains this?","choices":["Attrition bias","Compliance bias","Length-time bias","Lead-time bias"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Screening catches more slow, favorable conditions, so the screened group looks better even if screening changed nothing. — 8 PAJ5512 Prevention MBF_2026sc.pptx, Slide 19"},{"q":"Which is an advantage of cohort studies?","choices":["Incidence can be established directly","Results are available within weeks","Rare diseases can be studied cheaply","Few participants need enrolling"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct, because following exposed and unexposed persons forward shows how many develop disease; exposure is also recorded without knowledge of the outcome. — 7 PAJ5512 Risk MBF 2026sc.pptx, Slide 14"},{"q":"Why might a sponsoring company contribute to publication bias?","choices":["It selects participants who are most likely to benefit","It insists that all its funded studies be published in full","It requires investigators to disclose conflicts of interest","It is less likely to support research unfavorable to its product"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct, and it sits alongside journal editor preference as the second force keeping results that show nothing out of the literature. — 1 PAJ5512 Introduction Bias Validity MBF_2026sc.pptx, Slide 46"},{"q":"What did the retracted vaccine and autism study demonstrate about undeclared interests?","choices":["The study was retracted purely because others could not replicate it","The conflict was declared but judged unimportant","An undisclosed payment and a competing patent lay behind the work","Peer review reliably prevents this kind of failure"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct: the author had been paid to advise lawyers representing parents, and had filed a patent for a competing vaccine without disclosing either. — 2 PAJ5512 Evidence MBF_2026sc.pptx, Slide 19"},{"q":"What is noted about determining external validity?","choices":["It is determined by the journal in which the study appears","It follows automatically once internal validity is established","It is established by the size of the sample alone","It is rarely determined from a single study"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct, and the practical consequence is that a result has to be reproduced in other settings and populations before it can be relied on outside the original one. — 1 PAJ5512 Introduction Bias Validity MBF_2026sc.pptx, Slide 51"},{"q":"What is the weakness of the mean?","choices":["It cannot be calculated for interval data","It ignores the number of observations","It is the same as the mode in all distributions","It can be affected by extreme outliers"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct, which is why the median, defined by position rather than magnitude, holds up better when a few values sit far from the rest. — 3 PAJ5512 Rates and Measurement MBF_2026sc.pptx, Slide 71"},{"q":"How is the probability of an event estimated from counts?","choices":["Times it does not occur divided by times it can occur","Times it occurs divided by times it can occur","Times it occurs divided by times it does not occur","Times it can occur divided by times it occurs"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct, and the result must lie between zero, meaning the event cannot occur, and one, meaning it will definitely occur. — 9 PAJ5512 Statistics Causation MBF_2026sc.pptx, Slide 4"},{"q":"What does a likelihood ratio above 10 indicate?","choices":["Moderate evidence to rule in","An indeterminate result","Strong evidence to rule in","Strong evidence to rule out"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct, with 5 to 10 giving moderate evidence to rule in, and the band from 0.5 to 2 being indeterminate. — 4 PAJ5512 Tests MBF 2026sc.pptx, Slide 44"},{"q":"Which is an epidemiologic function of surveillance?","choices":["Publishing systematic reviews of the literature","Setting the price of preventive services","Detecting outbreaks and epidemics","Assigning treatment to individual patients"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct, alongside determining the magnitude of a problem, understanding natural history, documenting geographic spread, testing etiologic hypotheses, evaluating control strategies and monitoring changes in an agent. — 3 PAJ5512 Rates and Measurement MBF_2026sc.pptx, Slide 35"},{"q":"Which of these is a suggested way to keep current?","choices":["Sales representatives","Advertising","Patients","Press releases"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct, and the others suggested are professional organizations, journal articles and colleagues. — 10 PAJ5512 Clinical Questions and Reviews MBF_2026sc.pptx, Slide 37"}]};
